INT. GRAND HOTEL LIBRARY, MONTE VERDE - NIGHT

Leather armchairs. A fire in a marble hearth. A ladder on a brass rail. Rain on tall windows. A decanter on a silver tray.

LADY ISABELLA CORVINO-HARTWELL (50s, emerald silk, a cigarette holder) pours two glasses of brandy. Across the room, MR. CORNELIUS BANKOLE-FAIRWEATHER (60s, tuxedo, white gloves) examines a leatherbound volume. And by the fire, DR. ANNELIESE VOSSBERG-KANE (40s, plain gray dress, a doctor's bag at her feet) warms her hands.

ISABELLA
Cornelius, you'll tire your eyes.

CORNELIUS
My eyes have survived worse than Boccaccio.

ISABELLA
Anneliese, you must be cold.

ANNELIESE
I am quite well, Lady Corvino.

Isabella carries a glass across the room and hands it to the doctor. Anneliese accepts it, sniffs it, and does not drink.

ISABELLA
You don't care for brandy?

ANNELIESE
I care for knowing what's in it.

A pause. The fire pops. Cornelius shuts his book.

CORNELIUS
Bella, our guest is being cautious.

ISABELLA
Our guest is being rude.

ANNELIESE
(setting the glass on the mantel)
Our guest is a physician. I've examined enough of your late husband's guests to know the habits of this house.

ISABELLA
How dreadfully dramatic.

ANNELIESE
He died on this carpet, Lady Corvino. Not in his bed.

CORNELIUS
Dr. Vossberg--

ISABELLA
^Anneliese--

They speak together, stop, glare at one another. Anneliese lifts her bag onto the sofa and opens it.

ANNELIESE
I've brought what's left of his glass. The one you didn't wash.

Isabella's cigarette holder trembles. Cornelius takes one step toward the doctor, then thinks better of it.

ISABELLA
You have no proof.

ANNELIESE
I have a vial. And a friend at the laboratory in Geneva who owes me a favor.

CORNELIUS
What do you want?

ANNELIESE
(quietly)
I want to know who poured the first glass.

Silence. Rain. Isabella walks to the window. Her reflection stares back, thin and white. Cornelius studies the floor. Anneliese closes the bag, snaps it shut.

ISABELLA
(to the glass)
It was never meant for him.

ANNELIESE
Then who?

Isabella turns. She looks, not at the doctor, but at Cornelius. He does not look up. His gloved hands close slowly into fists.

CORNELIUS
Bella.

ISABELLA
You told me it was a sleeping draught.

CORNELIUS
It was.

ISABELLA
For him.

CORNELIUS
For him.

ISABELLA
Then why is the doctor here?

CORNELIUS
Because she was always going to be here.

He lifts his eyes to Anneliese, and there is nothing in them but a long, tired courtesy.

ANNELIESE
Mr. Bankole-Fairweather. Pour yourself a brandy.

He crosses to the decanter. Pours with perfect steadiness. Raises the glass. Looks at the fire. Drinks. The glass slips from his hand and shatters on the marble.

ANNELIESE (CONT'D)
(not moving)
I told you. I care about what's in it.

Isabella sinks into an armchair. The fire cracks. A discreet knock. BUTLER AUGUSTUS PELLEGRINI-OSEI (60s, immaculate, expressionless) enters with a fresh log, sees the shards, sees the body, and sets the log down very gently.

AUGUSTUS
Shall I send for the constable, my lady, or for the undertaker?

ISABELLA
(not looking up)
Both, Augustus. In that order.
